window.SNDSTATION_DOCS = [{"u":"index.html","t":"sndstation documentation","s":"","h":["sndstation documentation","Two places for docs","For users","For developers","For people who build with coding assistants","Writing a page"],"x":"sndstation documentation sndstation turns small Linux handhelds into music machines. Shops sell these units as cheap game consoles, and many end up in a drawer. Each one holds a capable ARM computer. sndstation is the ecosystem that puts it to work for musicians: an OS image, or a package for a Debian system a unit already runs, with a launcher, an SDK, plugins, and updates. David Viens at Plogue started the project in 2026. Status: private beta. No unit has passed its checks, no image is public, and the API is 0.x. This repository is private until the docs are ready. Two places for docs This repository holds the guides, sorted by who reads them: users, developers, and people who build with coding assistants. Most are stubs today. Each stub says what it will cover and where to look until then. sndstation/sndstation holds the reference that sits next to the code: the SDK, the launcher, and each subsystem. This repository links to those pages and does not copy them. For users Find a supported unit. sndstation runs on RK3326, RK3566, H700, and A133P handhelds. A shop listing names the model, not the chip: supported hardware lists the models, and Identifying your unit will say how to tell which one you hold. To choose one to buy, the device guide lists the units we have validated and ranks the rest on paper; clone this repository and open that file in a browser. Get a microSD card of your own. Do not flash the card the unit came with. Put it aside as it is: it boots the stock firmware, so it shows whether a fault is in the hardware or in what you flashed. For which card to buy, and how to check it for a fake, we defer to Handheld Index's guide to SD cards . Flash the image for your unit with balenaEtcher : pick the image, pick the card, flash. For the writing itself we defer to balena's instructions and support. Which image a unit takes is in Getting an image . During the private beta no image is public. An image is not the only way in. The platform also installs as a .deb onto an equivalent \"Lite\" Debian trixie system: the stock Raspberry Pi OS Lite images and Armbian are that. Switchroot's L4T-based Linux for the Switch is on Ubuntu 24.04, which is close enough. See Installing on a handheld . 4. Put the card in and switch on. The unit boots to the launcher. Set up the network in SETTINGS → Network: Wi-Fi where the unit has it, or USB networking to a computer where it does not. 5. Make music. The user guide is a stub; until it is written, the launcher describes every screen. For developers A CLAP plugin built for Linux arm64 against Debian trixie's glibc loads on a unit as it is. The launcher finds it, checks it, and rates it ( CLAP plugins ). To go further, pick a route: Write an app. Writing an app takes examples/hello from a copy on your computer to a unit's launcher. The quickstart is the short version, and the headers are in the API reference . Give a plugin the unit's panel and controls. embedded-hardware.md describes the host side of the clap.embedded-hardware extension. A plugin that uses it does not depend on sndstation's code. Publish packages. Publishing a vendor source . Guides: Setting up a unit for development Building on macOS with Docker Optimizing for small ARM chips Building on a Linux desktop, on macOS, and on a handheld is in Installation . For people who build with coding assistants sndstation does nothing special for coding assistants. An app written with one uses the same SDK, the same docs, and the same units as any other. We use these tools ourselves. What we do with their output is question it, and hold it to the standard of any developer on the team: a person reads every line, runs it, and measures it on a unit. Shipping an app with a coding assistant , a stub. AGENTS.md is what an assistant reads before it works in the sndstation repository. A change you send to sndstation itself follows the AI use policy : you are its editor. Writing a page Docs are in American English and follow the rules in sndstation's AI_POLICY.md and .vale.ini : plain sentences, no filler, and facts someone measured. Start a how-to from the guide template . A stub keeps its Stub note until the page is written.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/getting-started/images.html","t":"Getting an image, and going back","s":"Getting started","h":["Getting an image, and going back","Where images come from","Which image each unit runs","Before you flash: keep the stock card","Flashing a card","Going back to the stock firmware","Problems"],"x":"Getting an image, and going back Draft for #168. Placeholders marked TODO(#236/#…) wait on a decision. #236 tracks each one, and the second number is the decision's closed issue. A private-beta tester runs sndstation from an SD card image. This page says where the image for each unit comes from, how to put it on a card, and how to go back to the unit's stock firmware. Where images come from Images are built in sndstation/distribution , a private repository. How an invitee gets one (read access to that repository, or an image handed over) is TODO(#236/#124). Whether the repository later opens is TODO(#236/#120). Do not look for a public download: none is published. Which image each unit runs Each unit runs the sndstation rootfs, ROCKNIX or its stock image. The choice per unit is TODO(#236/#124). What the docs record: Slot Unit Image 1 Anbernic RG351V TODO(#236/#124) 2 R36S Blue and R40XX TODO(#236/#124) 3 XU10 TODO(#236/#124) 4 Powkiddy RGB30 TODO(#236/#124) 5 Anbernic RG40XX H TODO(#236/#124). Its LED rings were verified under ROCKNIX ( stick-leds.md ) 6 TRIMUI Smart Pro TODO(#236/#124) 7 Anbernic RG35XX SP needs two : the sndstation rootfs (the lid is evdev) and a stock BSP image (where hallkey can be read). Which BSP image: TODO(#236/#124) 8 Raspberry Pi 4, OS Lite TODO(#236/#124) The sndstation rootfs is a Debian system. Where one is not offered for a unit, you can install the platform onto a Debian or Ubuntu image the unit already runs: see Installation . Before you flash: keep the stock card Flashing overwrites the card. To be able to go back: Keep the original card and flash a second one. This is the safest. Or image the stock card first , with the card in a reader. On Linux, after finding the device as below: sh sudo dd if=/dev/sdX of=stock-backup.img bs=4M status=progress Keep stock-backup.img somewhere safe. It is as large as the card. Whether any of the eight boots from internal storage rather than the card is TODO(#236/#124). This page assumes the card. Flashing a card Check the device name twice. dd writes to whatever it is told to, and a wrong name overwrites a disk you want. Look at the size and the label. If you would rather not use a terminal, a graphical tool such as balenaEtcher writes an image to a card and checks it. Pick the image, pick the card, flash. With dd : # Linux: find the card; it is the one that matches your card's size lsblk sudo dd if=the-image.img of=/dev/sdX bs=4M conv=fsync status=progress # macOS diskutil list diskutil unmountDisk /dev/diskN sudo dd if=the-image.img of=/dev/rdiskN bs=4m If the image is compressed, decompress it first. The image's format is TODO(#236/#124). When it finishes, eject the card, put it in the unit and switch on. A working install boots to the launcher: see Installation, Verifying . Going back to the stock firmware Put the unit's original card back, or reflash the vendor's stock image onto a card: Get the stock image from the vendor's own firmware page for that model. Do not use a copy of unknown origin. Flash it as above, or write back your stock-backup.img . Boot. The unit is as it was. This assumes the unit boots from the card. If it boots from internal storage, going back differs: TODO(#236/#124). Slot 7 (RG35XX SP) needs a stock BSP image anyway, for the hallkey reading; keep it. Problems If a unit does not boot after flashing, reflash the card, and try a second card if it still fails. A hardware result, good or bad, goes to reporting.md . Known problems are in known-issues.md .","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/getting-started/installation.html","t":"Installation","s":"Getting started","h":["Installation","From flashing a card to the launcher","Building from source","Installing on a handheld","Verifying"],"x":"Installation Draft for #169. Placeholders marked TODO(#236/#…) wait on a decision. #236 tracks each one, and the second number is the decision's closed issue. From flashing a card to the launcher For private-beta testers. You need a supported unit ( supported.md ) and an SD card. Get the image for your unit and keep the stock card or image it first: images.md . Flash the card and put it in the unit: images.md . Switch on. The unit boots into the launcher on its own screen: the install sets tty1 to log the user in and run sndstation-session ( launcher.md , \"Booting into it\"). Check it as in Verifying . On the first run, the launcher asks to map the pad if it does not know it, and to set up the sound by ear. Both can be skipped and done later from Controls and Audio. Report what you see , good or bad: reporting.md . If the image already carries sndstation, you are done. If you started from a plain Debian or Ubuntu image, install the package as in Installing on a handheld . What the install does as root is listed in deb-root.md . Going back to the stock firmware is in images.md . Whether a given image carries sndstation already is TODO(#236/#124). Building from source For developers. The third-party libraries in third_party/ are git submodules, so clone with them, or fetch them into a checkout you already have (and again after a pull that moves one): git clone --recurse-submodules <url> git submodule update --init --recursive # in an existing checkout Debian or Ubuntu, on the handheld itself or a desktop: sudo apt install build-essential cmake pkg-config libsdl2-dev libsdl2-ttf-dev libsdl2-image-dev libasound2-dev cmake -S . -B build cmake --build build -j4 ./build/sndstation # the launcher, in a 640x480 window on a desktop Tested with SDL 2.0.20 (Ubuntu 22.04, the oldest it is built on) and SDL 2.30 (Ubuntu 24.04). alsa-lib ( libasound2-dev , which libsdl2-dev pulls in anyway) gives the audio stream, the mixer, and MIDI their ALSA side; built without it, sound is SDL's and MIDI has no devices. On macOS, for development: brew install pkg-config sdl2 sdl2_ttf sdl2_image , then the same cmake lines. A desktop build is fully usable for development. The keyboard stands in for the gamepad (arrows, Z/X, Return, Backspace), and --fake-audio rk817 answers as a handheld's sound card. --fake-net gives SETTINGS → Network an in-memory unit: networks to join, Bluetooth devices to pair, and USB networking and SSH to turn on. ./build/sndstation --shots DIR writes every screen as a BMP without a display. Tests, git hooks, and the pre-push checks are in CONTRIBUTING . Installing on a handheld A tagged release ( v0.12.0 , …) carries two packages for arm64 units, built on Ubuntu 22.04. They install on Ubuntu 22.04 and later and Debian bookworm and later. Releases up to 0.15.0 were built on Debian trixie and need trixie or later: sndstation_<version>_arm64.deb , the platform; sndstation-vendors_<version>_all.deb , the vendors a unit can trust. How an invitee downloads them is TODO(#236/#120/#122). Copy both to the unit and install them together: sudo apt install ./sndstation_*_arm64.deb ./sndstation-vendors_*_all.deb To package a checkout instead, run packaging/make-deb.sh on the unit. It builds for the system it runs on and writes both packages to dist/ : packaging/make-deb.sh # builds, then packages both into dist/ sudo apt install ./dist/sndstation_*.deb ./dist/sndstation-vendors_*.deb The sndstation package recommends sndstation-vendors but does not need it. That package holds the vendors SETTINGS → Software packages offers to trust ( updates.md , \"Vendors\"). Without it the launcher works, and that page lists no vendors. On a recognized handheld the install also sets the unit up to boot into the launcher: - a realtime allowance for the audio thread; - a udev rule for the stick lights; - tty1 autologin into the session; - a sudo rule that lets SETTINGS → Network run its one network helper as root. Anywhere else it changes nothing outside /usr/share/sndstation . docs/launcher.md has the details and the switches ( SNDSTATION_BOOT=no , SNDSTATION_USER=… ). Verifying A working install on a handheld: It boots to the launcher on tty1 , with no login prompt, showing the APPS page. L1 and R1 change the page: APPS, PLUGINS, SETTINGS, ABOUT. ABOUT shows the unit. It lists the platform's version, the board, the SOC ID, the kernel, CPU, memory, SDL, the display, and the input device. The SOC ID is how the unit is told from another of its model ( unit-identity.md ). The pad works. A unit whose pad is not in the gamepad database opens Controls on a first-run card. With no pad found at all the launcher says \"No gamepad found\" and leaves to a shell after five seconds. Exit to shell ends the session and leaves a prompt. It is the last item of the power menu: press MENU (the unit's FN, Home, or MENU key), or hold SELECT and press START ( launcher.md , \"The power menu\"). Logging out logs in again, and the launcher returns. From a shell on the unit (ssh will do), after logging in again so that pam_limits has run: dpkg -s sndstation | head -3 # the package is installed, and its version groups # audio, video, input (and dialout for the stick lights) ulimit -r # 95: the audio thread may run realtime The install script says which user it set up and warns if that user is missing from audio , video or input ( launcher.md , \"Booting into it\"). A realtime refusal is said on stderr and shown on AUDIO / DIAGNOSTICS, row Thread ( rt-bench.md , \"Allowing it\"). On a unit with stick lights, sndstation --led-test lights the rings in turn ( stick-leds.md ). On a desktop, ./build/sndstation opens a 640x480 window, and ctest --test-dir build runs the tests ( CONTRIBUTING ). Nothing else is installed, and nothing boots into it. If something is wrong, see known-issues.md .","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/getting-started/quickstart.html","t":"Quickstart","s":"Getting started","h":["Quickstart","1. Run the launcher on a desktop","2. Write an app","3. Next steps"],"x":"Quickstart Draft for #169. This page is for people writing an app. To put sndstation on a unit and try it, start with Installation . 1. Run the launcher on a desktop After building : ./build/sndstation The keyboard stands in for the pad (arrows, Z/X, Return, Backspace). --fake-audio rk817 pretends to be a handheld's sound card. 2. Write an app Start from examples/hello : a complete app in one file. It opens the screen, reads the pad, plays a sound and quits back to the launcher, and its README walks through it. An app is an ordinary program. It is listed by the launcher when its package installs a manifest, /usr/share/sndstation/apps/<id>.json : { \"name\": \"Example\", \"description\": \"What it is, in a line\", \"exec\": [\"/opt/example/example\"], \"order\": 10 } When the user picks it, the launcher shuts itself down and execs it, so the app has the display, the audio device and every byte of memory to itself. When it exits, the session brings the launcher back. To use the SDK, add this tree to your build and link the library: add_subdirectory(path/to/sndstation ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/sndstation) target_link_libraries(myapp PRIVATE sndstation::sndstation) The SDK is a library, not a framework: your program owns its main loop and calls in. Its headers are included as <sndstation/…> , and <sndstation/sndstation.h> includes them all. Everything in them is in namespace sndstation ( sndstation::pad , sndstation::gfx , sndstation::AudioOut , …); qualify it, or write using namespace sndstation; in your own files. The headers are listed in the API reference . The platform's settings are shared by every app on the unit: the gamepad database, stick calibration, and the unit's audio. For audio, that is where it plays, the routes, each output's volume and maximum, and the rate and buffer size. The launcher writes them; an app reads them through the SDK and keeps its own settings in ~/.config/<YourApp> . 3. Next steps Concepts overview API reference","stub":false}];
